    <div class="prose max-w-none">
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-8">ChatGPT and Claude can cut hours from the work that piles up in a manufacturer's marketing department: product descriptions, spec sheet copy, LinkedIn posts, distributor emails and first drafts in Spanish. They can also put a wrong rating on a product page in seconds. The difference is how you use them.</p>
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl mb-4">ChatGPT or Claude: which one should a marketer use?</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-8">Both are general-purpose AI assistants. They write, rewrite, summarize and work with documents you give them, and both release new versions every few months, so any ranking you read today will be outdated soon.</p>
      <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-8">The practical answer: test both on three real tasks from your own week, for example a product description from a spec table, a summary of a 20-page manual and a reply to a distributor question. Keep the one whose output needs fewer edits for your products. Many teams end up using both, one to draft and the other to review.</p>
      <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-8">What matters more than the brand is the account. Use a business plan or whatever your company approves, so product data, pricing and customer names stay under your company's data policy.</p>
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl mb-4">Where AI saves the most time in industrial marketing</h2>
      <ul class="checklist mb-8">
        <li><span>First drafts of product descriptions from a spec table or datasheet.</span></li>
        <li><span>Turning a new spec sheet into a LinkedIn post, a line card blurb or a distributor email.</span></li>
        <li><span>Summarizing a long installation manual into a one-page quick reference.</span></li>
        <li><span>Cleaning up a messy SKU spreadsheet into consistent attribute names and units.</span></li>
        <li><span>Rewriting answers to the questions distributors and contractors ask every week.</span></li>
        <li><span>First-pass Spanish drafts that a native speaker then reviews with your glossary.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl mb-4">Prompts that work for technical content</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-8">Give the AI the source document, the audience and the format. Vague prompts get generic copy.</p>
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-gray-100 p-6 mb-8" style="background:var(--surf)">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong>Product description:</strong> "Using only the specs in the table below, write a 120-word product description for electrical contractors. Lead with the application, then the three specs they filter by. Do not add any rating, certification or dimension that is not in the table."</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong>Spec sheet to post:</strong> "Here is our new spec sheet. Write a LinkedIn post for purchasing managers at distributors: what changed, who it is for, and a line inviting them to download the PDF. No hype words."</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed"><strong>Spanish draft:</strong> "Translate this installation guide into Spanish for warehouse crews in Florida. Use the terms in the attached glossary exactly. Mark any term you are not sure about with [CHECK]."</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl mb-4">What AI gets wrong with technical products</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-8">AI writes with the same confidence whether it is right or wrong. With industrial products, the common mistakes are:</p>
      <ul class="checklist mb-8">
        <li><span>Inventing specs, load ratings or certifications (a UL listing or NSF approval the product does not have).</span></li>
        <li><span>Mixing units or rounding numbers that should be exact.</span></li>
        <li><span>Translating trade terms literally, so the Spanish reads correctly but nobody on the floor uses those words.</span></li>
        <li><span>Sounding sure about compliance questions it cannot answer for your facility.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-8">The rule that prevents most problems: every number and claim gets checked against the source datasheet before it is published, and anything about safety or compliance is approved by the person responsible for it in your company.</p>
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl mb-4">Where AI fits in your marketing</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-8">AI speeds up drafts. It does not replace the system around them: consistent <a href="/en/solutions/spec-sheets/" style="color:var(--primary)">spec sheet templates</a>, clean <a href="/en/solutions/product-content/" style="color:var(--primary)">product data</a>, a reviewed <a href="/en/solutions/technical-translation/" style="color:var(--primary)">Spanish version</a> and someone who knows the products. Teams that get the most out of it use AI for the first 70 percent and spend their own time on the part that needs product knowledge.</p>
      <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-8">If you want AI working inside your sales process, answering questions and following up on quotes in English and Spanish, see our <a href="/en/solutions/ai-automation/" style="color:var(--primary)">bilingual AI automation</a>.</p>
